<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST11903"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><a href="mailto:bmodule_244@hotmail.com" target="_blank"><!--/email--><!--name-->Bmodule<!--/name--></b></a>
</a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Bmodule<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Sunday, March 20, 2005 - 6:44 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->You may be right Tac. I was thinking that they were probably recent vets trying to start up a nest egg or something. <BR> <BR>Did the guys you&#39;re talking about fly all the way to Wichita though. It seems, however, that they were more reckless than I had originally thought. <BR> <BR>The small aircraft into small airstrip is where I was thinking that Otero might fit in nonetheless. Imagine these guys having an arrangement at cook field, someone would be paid to back them up and provide a cover story to explain the plane&#39;s coming and going, and everyone else either didn&#39;t care or knew better than to interfere. To keep an airplane flying at all requires a minimal amount of maintenance, and unlike cars, all maintenance must be signed off on. <BR> <BR>Suppose now, that a new mechanic shows up and asks questions, refuses to sign off on an airworthiness certificate for a crappy plane, maybe even seems like he might make some phone calls or something. <BR> <BR>All in all, my whole point of looking into the plane wreck is to see if Otero could have run afoul of these people in his line of work. I was thinking amateurs because the Otero crime scene was too &#34;personal&#34; and at that time some smuggling was still being done by hippies for &#34;smoking profit&#34;, however it was also the transitional period when the cartels were forming also, everything was less peace and love and more &#34;I got mine&#34;. <BR> <BR>Did any of your books make any mention of an incident similar to the one we are looking at? If so, and it mentions names &#40;or aliases, nicknames&#41;, that is valuable information. The facts of the actual crash and/or type of craft may have been altered to protect the guilty, but the general place &#40;Miami&#41;and time period &#40;73-75&#41;would be about the same.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 11903-->
<!--Post: 11949--><!--Time: 1111444649-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST11949"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Tac<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Tac<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->11-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Monday, March 21, 2005 - 2:37 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->B, <BR> <BR>These guys were far far more fearless and reckless than you could imagine. Long and Jung both thought they could fly and nearly lost their lives trying to be pilots. They finally hired pros to fly the loads. BTW no outside maintenance man was ever hired to work their planes. Everything was in house so no word leaked. <BR> <BR>Jung &amp; Lehder&#39;s first airshipment was a $3mil Coke deal in late 1976 with their pilot Barry Kane, a well known Mass lawyer, as the pilot. Kane got $1.8mil and J &amp; L ea got $600k. Other players in this first group were Delbert Lapham, Brian Whittaker &amp; David Mahan.  <BR> <BR>The timeframe of 1973-75 would have been exclusively pot smugglers. Pot smuggling was said to be conducted with a handshake while coke deals were conducted with a gun.  <BR> <BR>In Long&#39;s book his main connection was a business man in Ann Arbor, Michigan named Jake Myerson. They were flying loads from Columbia all the way into Ann Arbor Michigan. They placed plastic &#34;bladder&#34; gas tanks, totally illegal by FAA standards as it increases a planes range, inside the fuselage increasing their range and eliminating stops. This was a common practice. What was not a common practice was that Long was the first to go with the giant DC-3.  .  .   <BR> <BR>Long came to know Jung and stated that at that time most smugglers knew one another. Jung between 1973-75 was flying pot in from Mazatlan, Mexico into the dry lake beds around Palm Springs.  <BR> <BR>Jung then loaded up 300-400 kilos of pot packed it into a Winnebago and drove it cross country to the Amherst, Mass area to sell to the multitudes of college kids in the area. <BR> <BR>Pilots of note: Frank Hatfield &amp; Will Mcbride partnered with Long and they were based in Florida. <BR> <BR>JD Reed. . .  Pilot &amp; roughneck worked for Long based in Vegas flew out of Florida. <BR> <BR>Lee Carlyle was based in Chicago and was a huge Pot dealer and pilot. He later partnered with Long on Coke deals. Carlyle was based in Florida after being run out of Chicago. <BR> <BR>I will try to get more names or info as I review the books.  .  .   <BR> <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 11949-->
